          "output": "Recommend ML-DSA-44 (Dilithium). Its signatures are about 2.4 KB, while\nSLH-DSA-128s signatures are roughly 7.9 KB; on a 200-byte LoRa frame the extra\nbandwidth and energy cost of SLH-DSA is significant for a battery-powered\nsensor. ML-DSA rests on module-lattice assumptions, which are less conservative\nthan SLH-DSA's reliance only on hash security, but the lattice assumptions\nbehind ML-KEM and ML-DSA are the best-studied post-quantum assumptions and are\ndeployed at scale. If bandwidth were abundant I would pick SLH-DSA for its\nminimal assumptions; here bandwidth and energy dominate. Both need constant-time\nimplementation care.\n"
